  "query_preview": "..\n\nNo significant weather expected today while a jet max ejects across \nThe UP and into Ontario. The occluded low pressure system stemming \nfrom this wave exhibits vertical continuity through the 300 mb level \nwhile the progressive surface reflection tracks from The Northwest \nAngle to James Bay between 12Z and 00Z today. Meanwhile, lower \ntropospheric theta-e advection begins to work into Southeast \nMichigan from the confluent southwesterly trajectories. The latest \nNCEP solutions continue to resolve nil QPF given the lack of \nprecipitation response upon arrival of the system's cold front. The \nfront is still expected to transition to a stationary boundary over \nLower Michigan, and will be met with ambient dryness through much of \nthe atmospheric column. A representative sampling of area forecast \nsoundings indicates broad dewpoint depressions and established \nstatic stability between the 5 kft and 15 kft AGL layers, attributed \nto the distant influence of Mid-Atlantic mid level ridging. PWATs \nwill rise, marked by the present increase in high cloud fraction and \nan attempt later today at some component of diurnal cumuliform \ncloud. Compared to the 19.00Z KDTX RAOB, PWATs increase by a factor \nof three this afternoon, from 0.22 to around 0.60 inches. However, \nthe lack of saturation precludes any potential for rain.\n\nMain forecast concern today remains focused on southwesterly wind \nmagnitudes as the CWA becomes positioned between strengthening \ngradient flow from the low to the northwest and surface high \npressure to the southeast. As discussed over the last several \nforecast cycles, the shallow mixing depths will limit the frequency \nand duration of peak gusts today. The strongest low level jet winds \nshould reside farther north with closer proximity to slightly cooler \nair. Areas north of M-46 stand the best chance to see gusts of 30+ \nmph this afternoon while gusts should generally hold below 25 mph \ntoward the south.\n\nRidging returns Friday with dry weather persisting into the weekend. \nAny westerly component to area winds drops off as the day \nprogresses, backing southerly by the afternoon hours as the surface \nanticyclone recenters over central Appalachia. Upstream, a Pacific \nlongwave trough works through the Four Corners area before gradually \nphasing with a meandering upper low moving south across the Canadian \nPrairies. As this unfolds, the eastern CONUS jet will strengthen, \nalthough split-flow should reside over/near Southeast Michigan into \nSaturday. Double digit 850 mb temperatures are becoming more likely \non Saturday resulting in widespread highs within the low to mid 60s. \nThe next chance for rain comes Sunday as the composite trough axis \nreleases across the Midwest. Latest medium range solutions have \nshown signs of deamplification upon reaching the Great Lakes, but \nthe baroclinic zone and kinematics favor a period of showers on \nSunday. Turning much colder post-frontal Monday with an arctic \nairmass spilling in from 1040 mb Saskatchewan/Manitoba high \npressure. Wind chills drop into the teens at times.",
